# Retrieved 2026-10-07; upstream commit da84378b36bd5b2e3cb35f610d64630bf1bd899d.
# Conversion note: Ingredients already say stewed gooseberries, method stews them; no oven temperature. Prepared ingredient state and first operation overlap.

::ingredients

(gooseberries, stewed, with all juice) 1 pint
(sugar; powdered) 1/4lb
(butter, fresh) 2oz
(bread, stale, grated) 2oz
(egg) 3
(puff-paste) enough to line a soup plate

::equipment

(spoon)
(grater)
(soup plate)
(oven)

::instructions

(gooseberries) <stew, until quite soft> <cool> <mash, finely with back of spoon>
    + (sugar) 2oz <stir>
{butter mixture} = (sugar) remaining 2oz + (butter) <cream>
(bread) <grate, very fine, weigh 2oz>
(egg) <beat>
{butter mixture} + (egg) + (gooseberries) + (bread) <stir, eggs alternating with gooseberries and bread>
(puff-paste) <line, soup plate>
    + {mixture} <fill> <bake, 30m, temperature not stated>
# Do not grate sugar over it.

::notes
Do not grate sugar over it.

::source
<<<
---
title: "Gooseberry Pudding"
tags: ['berries', 'dessert', 'pudding']
date: 1832-01-01
author: "Miss Leslie"
---

## Ingredients

- A pint of stewed gooseberries, with all their juice.
- A quarter of a pound of powdered sugar.
- Two ounces of fresh butter.
- Two ounces of grated bread.
- Three eggs.

## Directions

1. Stew the gooseberries till quite soft. When they are cold, mash them fine with the back of a spoon, and stir into them two ounces of sugar. Take two ounces more of sugar, and stir it to a cream with two ounces of butter.
2. Grate very fine as much stale bread as will weigh two ounces.
3. Beat three eggs, and stir them into the butter and sugar, in turn with the gooseberries and bread.
4. Lay puff-paste in a soup plate. Put in the mixture, and bake it half an hour.

Do not grate sugar over it.
>>>
